    "final_report": "REAL: two distinct models; statistics computed with Python.\n\nMethod: paired-samples t-test (two-sided, second minus first)  (data version v1; Python: scipy.stats.ttest_rel(y, x, nan_policy='raise', alternative='two-sided'); confidence_interval(confidence_level=0.95); difference = y minus x)\nH0: μd = 0 versus H1: μd ≠ 0, where d = drug_2 − drug_1, one independent pair per row; α = 0.05\n\nComplete independent pairs: n = 10; each row pairs drug_1 with drug_2.\nMean difference (drug_2 minus drug_1) = 1.58 (95% CI 0.70 to 2.46); SD of differences = 1.23; t(9) = 4.062; p = 0.0028\nDecision at α = 0.05: reject_H0\nDistribution checks (descriptive only, never used to choose the method): [{'check': 'shapiro_wilk', 'variable': 'drug_2 minus drug_1', 'role': 'paired_difference', 'n': 10, 'shapiro_wilk_p': 0.03334160863030771, 'note': 'Describes the within-pair differences, not either marginal distribution; does not establish normality or independence of pairs.'}]\n\nWe analysed 10 complete independent pairs of drug_1 and drug_2. The paired t-test result is t(9) = 4.062, p = 0.0028. At 0.05, we reject H0, which states that the mean difference (drug_2 minus drug_1) is 0. The estimated mean difference is 1.58, with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.70 to 2.46, indicating a positive change. Pairing alone does not establish causation.\n\nLimitations:\n- Each row must be a correctly matched pair and pairs must be independent. The paired t-test assumes approximately normal within-pair differences for small samples; descriptive normality checks do not establish this assumption.\n- Random allocation was not established. This analysis describes an association and does not establish causation.\n- Inference is conditional on the stated independent observations and the method's distribution and model assumptions; descriptive checks do not prove these assumptions.",
